  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe cold front that slipped southward through the area last\nnight and early this morning will buckle tonight and Friday,\npushing farther south east of the mountains, setting up cold \nair damming, and returning north as a warm front across the Tug\nFork and into the middle Ohio Valley.\n\nThe cold air damming will just lower temperatures and keep\nlow stratus entrenched beneath a low-level inversion developing\nas a result in and especially along the eastern slopes of the \nmountains. To the west, the clouds may lift and break up a bit.\n\nRain across all but the northwest quarter to third of the\nforecast area will diminish this evening, as a flat wave along\nthe front and mid-level flat wave go by. A mid/upper-level ridge\ncrosses overnight and early Friday morning.\n\nOn Friday, another mid/level flat wave and the front bring\nanother round of showers, with thunderstorms also becoming\npossible in the afternoon, especially near the warm front over \nthe middle Ohio Valley. The Storm Prediction Center has a \nMarginal Risk for Severe Thunderstorms for Friday afternoon and \nearly evening, with damaging wind and Tornadoes being the main \nthreat, the latter with low-level warm advection turning amid \nthe low LCL near the bottom of the warm front.\n\nAfter a lull in precipitation coverage overnight Friday night,\nthe next wave brings showers Saturday into Saturday night, with\nafternoon and evening thunderstorms possible. Thunderstorms in\nwestern or at least southwest portions of the forecast area can\nbecome strong and heavy, as a pseudo-cold front approaches,\nalthough the Storm Prediction Center just has southern portions\nof the area pegged for general thunderstorms for Saturday \nafternoon and early evening.\n\nThe next mid/upper-level short wave trough lifts to our north \nand west Sunday and Sunday night, and then heights build, with a\nsouthern stream short wave possibly meandering and getting lost\nto our west. However, each may sneak nearby enough Monday for \nimpactful afternoon and evening shower and thunderstorm \ncoverage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nFor the short work week to follow, a ridge building to our \nnorth at all levels and the surface boundary gets left behind \nwith nothing to push it along one way or another, although \nmid/upper-level northerly flow on the east side of the ridge may\neventually push it southward. As such, shower and thunderstorm \ncoverage decreases and becomes increasingly diurnal in nature. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nAfter a cool afternoon, temperatures climb to near normal Friday\nand Saturday, then just a few degrees above normal for next \nweek, except back close to normal for Memorial Day itself.",
